content:
We need to produce final answer JSON route. Since schema validation expects root with one reaction child, we must format accordingly.

Construct route JSON:

{
 "type":"mol",
 "smiles":"Cc1ccc(NS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)Nc3ccccc3N)cc2)cc1",
 "children":[
   {
     "type":"reaction",
     "template_id":1,
     "children":[
       {
         "type":"mol",
         "smiles":"Cc1ccc(NS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)O)cc2)cc1",
         "children":[
           {
             "type":"reaction",
             "template_id":2,
             "children":[
               {
                 "type":"mol",
                 "smiles":"Cc1ccc(NS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(C=O)cc2)cc1",
                 "children":[
                   {
                     "type":"reaction",
                     "template_id":3,
                     "children":[
                       {"type":"mol","smiles":"Cc1ccc(S(=O)(=O)Cl)cc1"},
                       {"type":"mol","smiles":"c1ccc(N)cc1"}
                     ]
                   }
                 ]
               },
               {"type":"mol","smiles":"C(C(=O)O)C(=O)O"}
             ]
           }
         ]
       },
       {"type":"mol","smiles":"c1ccc(N)cc1"} // Wait this is same as used earlier; but need aniline for amide: c1ccc(N)cc1 is aniline, but we already used that for sulfonamide. Actually amide uses c3ccccc3N (aniline). Use same SMILES "c1ccc(N)cc1" is generic aniline. That's fine.
     ]
   }
 ]
}

But note we used aniline both as leaf for amide and as sulfonamide nucleophile; they are same SMILES, okay.

Now verify route.
